name: structs and enums
intro: A struct groups named fields into one value. `typedef` gives it a
intro: short name, and a designated initializer fills it by field, so
intro: order does not matter and anything left out is zero:
intro:
intro:     typedef struct {
intro:         char name[16];
intro:         long score;
intro:     } player;
intro:
intro:     player p = { .score = 40 };
intro:     p = (player){ .score = 41 };   /* compound literal */
intro:
intro: The other form gives the struct a tag instead, and the type is then
intro: written `struct point`, with the word struct every time:
intro:
intro:     struct point { long x; long y; };
intro:     struct point p = { .x = 3, .y = 4 };
intro:     static long dist2(const struct point *p);
intro:
intro: Same thing; typedef just saves typing the word. Either is fine.
intro:
intro: Structs copy by value, so a big one passed to a function is copied
intro: whole. Pass a pointer instead, and `const` when it only reads:
intro:
intro:     static long total(const player *v, size_t n);
intro:     p.score          /* field of a value */
intro:     q->score         /* field through a pointer, same as (*q).score */
intro:
intro: An enum names a set of constants. Switch over one WITHOUT a
intro: `default:` and the compiler warns when you forget a case -- that
intro: warning is worth more than the default (gcc and clang warn about a
intro: missing case; tcc does not.) Put a return after the switch, since
intro: tcc here makes "might return no value" an error:
intro:
intro:     enum op { OP_ADD, OP_SUB };
intro:     switch (op) {
intro:     case OP_ADD: return a + b;
intro:     case OP_SUB: return a - b;
intro:     }
intro:     return 0;
intro:
intro: Each case ends in `return` or `break`; without one, execution
intro: falls into the next case. An array of structs makes a table: a
intro: fixed list of records you loop over with sizeof arr / sizeof arr[0].
intro: Each row is its own braces, and an enum is a type you can use for a
intro: field, a variable or a parameter:
intro:
intro:     enum color { RED, GREEN };
intro:     struct paint { const char *name; enum color c; };
intro:     static const struct paint shelf[] = {
intro:         { .name = "apple", .c = RED },
intro:         { .name = "lime", .c = GREEN },
intro:     };
intro:     enum color want = shelf[1].c;      /* GREEN */
